// Rackelhanen: Kenneth Boström's poly-yarn caddis, a Swedish classic since 1967.

id rackelhanen
name "Rackelhanen"
aliases "Rackelhahn" "Polysedge" "Honk"
type dry
imitates caddis
naturals caddis-adult caddis-pupa
difficulty beginner
species trout grayling char
season june july august september
origin originator="Kenneth Boström" year=1967 region="Vänneån, Sweden"
description "A shaggy body of chopped poly yarn, fuller at the rear, a double poly-yarn wing folded over the back, and a dubbed front body."
history """
    Kenneth Boström tied it in 1967 during a caddis hatch on the Vänneån in southern Sweden,
    where the fish took only caddis hatching just under the surface. He thought it ugly and
    was slow to show it to his friends. One of them named it after the rackelhane, the hybrid
    of a black grouse and a capercaillie, because it looked like a "bastard". Boström does not
    name the friend; Lars Chr. Bentsen (Ahrex), who heard the story from Boström, says it was
    Lennart Bergqvist, and that Boström had called it Boström's Sedge.
    """
fishing """
    Always treat it with floatant. Boström's four ways: short, nervous strips for egg-laying
    caddis; without wings as a swimming pupa; pulled under and let go so it pops up like a
    hatching caddis; or dead drift as a spent caddis. Pulled under, it trails bubbles from
    the air in its wing. Kasper Mühlbach uses it for grayling in fast water, where it stays
    visible even in the light summer nights.
    """
sources {
    "Rackelhanen (Kenneth Boström)" url="https://www.rackelhane.se/eng/10019.htm"
    "GlobalFlyFisher: Rackelhanen (Kasper Mühlbach)" url="https://globalflyfisher.com/patterns/rackelhanen"
    "Ahrex: The Rackelhanen (Lars Chr. Bentsen)" url="https://ahrexhooks.com/2020/07/18/the-rackelhanen/"
}
// Photos for checking proportions while authoring (pnpm refcheck); never published.
references {
    "https://www.rackelhane.se/images/rack6a.jpg" page="https://www.rackelhane.se/eng/10019.htm" author="Kenneth Boström" license="All rights reserved; reference only" note="The originator's fly, side view"
    "https://globalflyfisher.com/sites/default/files/imported_pictures/IMG_3548.jpg" page="https://globalflyfisher.com/patterns/rackelhanen" author="Kasper Mühlbach" license="All rights reserved; reference only" note="Side view, light ginger"
    "https://ahrexhooks.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/Rackelhanen_Dark.jpg" page="https://ahrexhooks.com/2020/07/18/the-rackelhanen/" author="Lars Chr. Bentsen" license="All rights reserved; reference only" flip=#true note="Side view, dark"
}

// Assumption: Damp, the poly body lies in the film with the wing tent over it (Boström; photos
// of poly-yarn sedges on the water).
sits body
hook dry-standard size=12
thread brown size="8/0"

materials {
    body dubbing color=brown label="Poly yarn, cut into 1 cm pieces and dubbed"
    wing poly-yarn color=brown
    front dubbing color=brown label="Poly yarn, cut into 1–2 cm pieces and dubbed"
}

steps {
    attach-thread at=0.9
    wrap-thread to=0.0
    dub body to=0.65 note="Touch-dub the fluffed yarn onto waxed thread; two turns back towards the bend, then forward, so the rear is fuller."
    tie-in wing length=1.45 angle=15 spread=105 note="Tied in by its middle, one half back, the other folded back over it; trimmed to end just behind the bend."
    dub front to=0.92
    whip-finish note="Tease out the body fibres for legs."
}
